.TH MOULD 1
.SH NAME
mould \- report walls, drafts and undercuts along a pull
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mould
.BR x | y | z
.IR stream | \-
[
.I pin ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Mould
reads a stream and prints, for each solid, what matters for a mould
that opens along the given axis:
.PP
.RS
.nf
part NAME
pull AXIS
wall thin T at X Y Z
wall thick T at X Y Z
side plus|minus faces N draft D
group plus|minus KEY faces N draft D
flat NAME
rim NAME
turn NAME
undercut NAME at X Y Z
pin NAME at X Y Z
.fi
.RE
.PP
The thin wall is the least distance through the solid along a normal;
the thick wall is the diameter of the largest ball inside it.
Each face belongs to the side toward which its normal leans.
Draft is the least angle between a face and the axis, in degrees,
and is given with a count of faces for each side and for each group
(the faces of one extrusion, or the rounds).
A face with no draft is reported as
.B flat
(parallel to the axis),
.B rim
(draft 0 at an edge only), or
.B turn
(leaning both ways).
An undercut is a face with a point from which the mould cannot be
drawn.
Undercuts whose names match a
.I pin
pattern (* for any run of characters) are reported as pins.
.SH SEE ALSO
part(5), fillet(1)
.SH BUGS
The solid is sampled at points of its faces only, so the thick wall is
accurate to about the square of the sampling step over the wall.
